\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. The class $S$ consists of holomorphic injective functions $f:\mathbb D\to\mathbb C$ normalized by $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$, so $f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_nz^n$. For $\Omega=f(\mathbb D)$ let $F:\Omega\to\mathbb D$ be the conformal inverse. The intersection $\Omega\cap\mathbb R$ can have multiple interval components; its integral means the one-dimensional Lebesgue integral over their union.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every $f\in S$ and every real $p$ with $1\le p<2$, is \[\int_{\Omega\cap\mathbb R}|F'(x)|^p\,dx<\infty?\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}
The source records the affirmative endpoint $p=1$ and slit-domain counterexamples at $p=2$. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is the inverse derivative integrable on the real part of every normalized univalent image for every exponent from one up to, but excluding, two?

\subsection{Sources}
